<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deganwy</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Deganwy is a site on twin volcanic hills above the estuary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-conwy">River Conwy</a> in north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, beside the modern town of the same name. Tradition links its early medieval fortress with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maelgwn-gwynedd">Maelgwn Gwynedd</a>, and excavation has found imported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> pottery of the 5th and 6th centuries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annales-cambriae">Annales Cambriae</a> record its destruction by the Mercians in 822, and a castle later built there by Henry III was destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-ap-gruffudd">Llywelyn ap Gruffudd</a> in 1263.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deganwy.jpg" alt="Deganwy" width="320" height="199" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NoelWalley at en.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deganwy7010254X.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
